==English==

===Etymology===
{{back-form|en|cathexis}} and {{m|en|cathectic}}. A [[loan creation]] {{coinage|en|Q1681142|nocap=1}} translating Freud’s {{der|en|de|besetzen}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈθɛkt/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛkt|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|psychology}} To focus one's emotional energies on someone or something.
#* '''1978''' [Simon & Schuster], {{w|M. Scott Peck}}, ''The Road Less Travelled'', 2012, Random House (Rider), [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=bRTZwrOni5EC&pg=PA105&dq=%22cathect%22%7C%22cathects%22%7C%22cathecting%22%7C%22cathected%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjflNPEg_z-AhU6IEQIHREAB6MQ6AF6BAgMEAI#v=onepage&q=%22cathect%22%7C%22cathects%22%7C%22cathecting%22%7C%22cathected%22&f=false page 105],
#*: The feeling of love is the emotion that accompanies the experience of '''cathecting'''. '''Cathecting''', it will be remembered, is the process by which an object becomes important to us. Once '''cathected''', the object, commonly referred to as a 'love object,' is invested with our energy as if it were part of ourselves, and this relationship between the us and the invested object is called a cathexis.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=Howard Kamler|title=Identification and Character|publisher=w:SUNY Press|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=MyLlMaAclvEC&pg=PA56&dq=%22cathect%22%7C%22cathects%22%7C%22cathecting%22%7C%22cathected%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjflNPEg_z-AhU6IEQIHREAB6MQ6AF6BAhYEAI#v=onepage&q=%22cathect%22%7C%22cathects%22%7C%22cathecting%22%7C%22cathected%22&f=false|page=56
|passage=Narcissism theorists talk about the work of the narcissism period being a "'''cathecting''' of the self." In fact, they should be talking about a "'''cathecting''' of the ''rudimentary'' self." Since all that gets '''cathected''' here are merely individually emerging ego self ''representations'' and not ego self ''structure''—the latter amounting to an internally integrated network of agency dispositions—it really makes no sense to talk about '''cathecting''' a self per se.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Human Emotions|page=193|author=Carroll E. Izard|year=2013|passage=Apparently it is possible for an individual to '''cathect''' any person, object, idea, or image. Of considerable importance to a possible analogy between cathexis and the emotion of interest, is Freud's notion that an individual can '''cathect''' thought or thinking as well as attention and perception.}}

